{
  "A": "Research a fast serial dense-DP kernel. Replace the two rolling rows and row-copy loop with one contiguous array: initialize dp[0]=1 and other entries to zero, then process every cell as blocked ? 0 : dp[j]+left modulo MOD. Since both operands are reduced, one conditional subtraction suffices and signed 32-bit addition remains safe. Compare compact blocked-cell storage against sorted per-row obstacle lists that split computation into obstacle-free spans; avoid unnecessary quadratic storage. Evaluate faster standard-input parsing. After establishing the scalar improvement, investigate ARM64 NEON inclusive prefix scans within obstacle-free spans, reducing after each addition stage and carrying the last value into the next vector. Preserve obstacle resets and scalar tails. Submit separately measured scalar and SIMD variants; do not assume either memory representation or SIMD wins.",
  "B": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle algorithm that avoids visiting all n² cells. Sort and deduplicate blocked coordinates, append the destination, and compute paths to each point by subtracting contributions through earlier coordinate-wise reachable blockers. Obtain unrestricted path counts from binomial coefficients using factorials and inverse factorials through 2n−2; the stated n bound keeps this below MOD. Use 64-bit modular products and normalize subtractions. Handle blocked endpoints and n=1 explicitly; an obstacle-free grid legitimately reduces to one binomial coefficient. Expected complexity is O(n + m log m + m²), so retain a general dense fallback rather than applying quadratic obstacle processing indiscriminately. Investigate workload-based crossover decisions using dimensions and effective obstacle count, not test identities or assumed obstacle distributions. Report preprocessing and end-to-end costs.",
  "C": "Research four-core CPU parallelism through cache-tiled wavefront DP, not a barrier for every matrix row. Process tiles on successive anti-diagonals, with north and west dependencies satisfied before each diagonal. Explore an O(n)-sized DP frontier plus O(n) row-edge storage: a tile updates its column segment using top values and saved left boundaries, then publishes bottom and right boundaries. Concurrent tiles must own disjoint frontier segments and row-edge ranges. Use one persistent parallel region where supported by the unchanged toolchain; tune rectangular tile sizes and scheduling under the fixed harness. Reuse the modular recurrence and compact obstacle handling without allocating a full integer DP matrix. Handle partial boundary tiles and preserve initialization exactly. Compare against the serial kernel and retain a serial path when parallel overhead dominates.",
  "G": "Integrate only correctness-validated programs from A, B and C, measuring standalone winners before combinations. First assess A's scalar and SIMD kernels, then C's tiled execution with compatible kernels, and finally B's sparse/dense dispatch. Do not assume their speedups add: sorting, representation conversion, vectorization and synchronization can interfere. Select algorithms using general workload properties and measured crossover evidence; avoid constructing representations that the selected algorithm will not use. Measure complete program runtime, including input, preprocessing and allocation, through the unchanged harness. Audit n=1, blocked endpoints, duplicate or unordered obstacles, empty obstacle sets, modular arithmetic and partial tiles. Preserve every valid input's result, leave tests, oracle, compiler, budgets and target unchanged, and retain the best measured standalone implementation if integration regresses.",
  "analysis": "The supplied source solves Problem D; A/B/C here are research roles, not the other contest problems. Its dense O(n²) recurrence uses a quadratic packed obstacle grid, two DP rows, a row-copy pass and a left-to-right dependency. Current evidence is only a correct baseline on four cases with three timings averaging 0.080334 seconds; neither input dimensions nor a bottleneck profile is supplied. Consequently, prioritize low-overhead serial improvements, an exact sparse algorithm, and coarse-grained parallelism as independent hypotheses. The target has four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU. The short baseline makes preprocessing and synchronization overhead important to measure. All candidates must preserve obstacle semantics and modular results across the full stated domain; no conclusion about speedup is justified until measured."
}